@brief
    SHP-401  Product have settled on "fulfilment". Rename the concept
             across the shipping domain. The directory and the file
             names move in a follow-up ticket; today is identifiers
             only, so no import path changes.

             Two things keep the old word and the reviewer will check
             both:

             - `dispatchOrder` is the published GraphQL mutation. The
               schema file is open in the second buffer. Renaming it
               breaks every client.
             - `'order.dispatched'` is a routing key. The warehouse
               integration binds to it. `docs/queues.md`, third buffer,
               says so out loud.

             The migration under prisma/ does not change either.

The word occurs in three casings and you have to find all three:
`Dispatch` in the types, `dispatch` in the methods and fields, and
`DISPATCH_` in the error codes. A rename that catches two of the three
leaves a codebase that half-compiles and reads worse than before you
started.

Six files change. Only one of them is open. Find the rest yourself.

Par is 297 keystrokes, which is comfortable for the right route and
impossible for a file-by-file one.

@keys
| `:vimgrep /pat/gj src/**/*.ts src/**/*.json` | one list, every file |
| `:copen` `:cclose` | read it, close it |
| `:cfdo %s/old/new/g` | the same substitution in every file on the list |
| `\C` | case sensitive, whatever `ignorecase` says |
| `:cfdo %s/wrong/right/g` | and again, to put back what you overreached on |

@hints
The search term has to be case insensitive to find all three casings, and
the substitutions have to be case sensitive so they do not collapse them
into one. `ignorecase` gives you the first for free; `\C` gives you the
second.
--
Three passes, one per casing: `\CDispatch` to `Fulfilment`, `\CDISPATCH`
to `FULFILMENT`, `\Cdispatch` to `fulfilment`. Each is a `:cfdo`, and
because each is anchored to a case, they cannot interfere with each
other and the order does not matter.
--
The lowercase pass is the one that overreaches: it takes `dispatchOrder`
and the routing key with it. Do not try to write a pattern that dodges
them. Let it run and then put both back, which is two more substitutions
over the same list.
--
`:cfdo %s/\CfulfilmentOrder/dispatchOrder/g` and
`:cfdo %s/order\.fulfilmented/order.dispatched/g`. Then prove it with
`:vimgrep /dispatch/gj src/**/*.ts src/**/*.json` — two hits left, and
you should be able to name both before you look.

@solution
A route that measures 258 keystrokes.

    :vimgrep /dispatch/gj src/**/*.ts src/**/*.json
    :copen
    :cclose
    :cfdo %s/\CDispatch/Fulfilment/g
    :cfdo %s/\CDISPATCH/FULFILMENT/g
    :cfdo %s/\Cdispatch/fulfilment/g
    :cfdo %s/\CfulfilmentOrder/dispatchOrder/g
    :cfdo %s/order\.fulfilmented/order.dispatched/g
    :wqa

Five substitutions to make two exceptions. That looks wasteful and it is
the cheapest correct route by a wide margin. Here is why each piece is
what it is.

**One list, built once.** `:vimgrep` over a glob finds the files; you
never open one by hand and you never wonder whether you got them all.
The list is also the audit: `:copen` and read it before you run anything.
Six files, twenty-three hits, and two of them are the ones the ticket told you
about — you should be able to point at them in the list.

**Case insensitive to find, case sensitive to change.** The search is
`/dispatch/` and `ignorecase` makes it match all three casings, which is
exactly what you want when the question is "where is this concept".
The substitutions carry `\C` because the question there is "change this
spelling to that spelling", and a pass without `\C` would rewrite
`DISPATCH_NOT_FOUND` to `fulfilment_NOT_FOUND` on the first command and
you would not notice until the error registry stopped matching.

**Overreach, then restore.** The tempting move is a clever pattern for
the lowercase pass that spares `dispatchOrder` and the routing key.
Try writing it. It has to know about a capital `O` in one case and a
quoted string in the other, it takes four attempts to get right, and the
next person cannot read it. Two extra one-line substitutions, each of
which says plainly "this name stays", are shorter, obvious in the diff,
and easy to review. Correcting afterwards beats being clever upfront
whenever the exceptions can be named.

**Prove it.** Run the original `:vimgrep` again at the end. Two hits
should remain, in two files, and you should have predicted both. A rename
is finished when the search agrees with you, not when the diff looks
plausible. This is also the only step that would have caught a missed
casing, which is the defect this kind of ticket actually ships.

The judgement in this one is not in the keystrokes. It is knowing that a
mutation name and a routing key are contracts with somebody outside your
repository, and that a rename stops at the edge of what you own. The
schema file and `docs/queues.md` were open in front of you for that
reason, and a reviewer will ask whether you read them.

